WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.629
Eighty-six percent.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.307
That is what the leading scanner says it finds in smart contracts, by its own count.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.669
But a percentage is only as good as the ruler that measured it.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.880
The official ruler is a judge. GPT-5, thinking hard, one call for every finding.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.075
We had written that it was out of reach. We were wrong. So we read its code.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:35.231
We rebuilt it, part by part. Same model, same settings, the same question for every finding.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.707
About three cents a call.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.351
Then we tested our ruler on the leader's own reports.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.499
Their count: twenty-six of thirty-one. Ours: twenty-five.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.200
The same verdict on thirty of thirty-one findings.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.789
Now the same forty reports, counted with two open judges.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.012
Seventy-six percent. Sixty-two. Fifty-five. Eighty-four.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.757
Same bugs. Same reports. A percentage without its judge says nothing.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.396
Our first sealed run found one of a hundred and five. We published it.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.224
Every mistake since is corrected in public, with the old text kept.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.304
Two hundred thirty-four judgments sit in an open folder. One command checks every count.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.759
Check it yourself.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.109
Tokenizen. Make security claims that anyone can check.

